    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-330609",
      "knowledge_point_id": "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain",
      "problem": "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies in the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add or subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot \\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$",
